fpc -Fu. -Fi. newVMbench.lpr}{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
 (default optimisation, no explicit -O3/-Sa - unlike perf/windows11ryzen9performance.rtf, which used -O3 -Sa-; not repeated here, so absolute PUREPASCAL times on this report may be a little pessimistic relative to a -O3 build, though the library-backed timings and the qualitative speedup pattern are unaffected either way since they're dominated by the library call itself, not this program's own code).}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang2057\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\widctlpar\hyphpar1\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\dbch\af10\langfe2052\ql\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb0\sa180\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
Backends detected by ./newvmconfigure: MKL not found, IPP not found, OpenBLAS found (libopenblas.so), Arm Performance Libraries found (libarmpl_lp64.so), FFTW3 (double+single) found. This machine is a new case for this report series: MKL and IPP are both absent (so PUREPASCAL stays }{\hich\af4\loch\i\f4\loch
active}{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
 for the VML/VSL/IPP-only routines this benchmark doesn't exercise - Sin/Cos/etc, fillRandom, linspace/AddScalar/FlipLR), but Arm Performance Libraries supplies a standard LAPACKE_* implementation alongside its cblas_* one, so the newly-added HAVE_LAPACKE define (independent of PUREPASCAL - see newvmconfigure.lpr's own comment) is active, and MatMult/LinearSolve/Invert/EigDecompose below are all genuinely library-backed via ArmPL, not PurePascal, in the "real" build. The PUREPASCAL comparison column was produced from a second build with newVMConfig.inc hand-forced to comment out HAVE_FFTW and HAVE_LAPACKE and define PUREPASCAL/PUREPASCAL_BLAS (same technique as the other platform reports in this folder), then regenerated back to the real per-machine config via ./newvmconfigure afterwards.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang2057\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\widctlpar\hyphpar1\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\dbch\af10\langfe2052\ql\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb0\sa180\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
Test suite verification: ./newVMtest (full 262-test FPCUnit suite, all five TVMobj*/TVMobjI types) - 262 run, 0 errors, 0 failures, against the real (library-backed) build, re-verified again after restoring the real config post-benchmark.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang2057\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\widctlpar\hyphpar1\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\dbch\af10\langfe2052\ql\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb0\sa180\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
Notes: Residuals are Frobenius-norm ||A*x-b|| (solve) / ||A*Ainv-I|| (invert) / round-trip ||IFFT(FFT(A))-A|| (FFT) / ||A*v-lambda*v|| worst-case over all eigenpairs (EigDecompose); both builds compute the same correct answer to within precision-appropriate tolerance in every case. Speedup = PUREPASCAL time / library-backed time (values below 1.00x mean the library-backed call was actually slower at that N). All timings in milliseconds, single run per row (not averaged).}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang2057\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\widctlpar\hyphpar1\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\dbch\af10\langfe2052\ql\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb0\sa180\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
N=10 first-call anomalies: several "first ArmPL/FFTW call of the process" cells are wildly out of line with their neighbours, the same phenomenon perf/windows11ryzen9performance.rtf documents for MKL/FFTW - e.g. Invert(real double) N=10 costs 22.43ms library-backed vs a near-zero 0.02ms PUREPASCAL (lapacke_dgetrf/dgetri is the first ArmPL LAPACKE code path this process touches that LinearSolve's earlier lapacke_dgesv call didn't already warm up); MatMultZ(double complex) N=10 costs 1.39ms vs 0.03ms for the same reason (first cblas_zgemm call); FFT(double) N=10 costs 0.15ms vs 0.03ms (FFTW's fftw_plan_dft_1d pays a one-time plan-creation cost on its first call); EigDecompose(double) N=10 costs 2.71ms vs 0.10ms (first LAPACKE_dgeev call). These cells are marked "n/a (1st call)" in the Speedup column rather than reported as a literal (and misleading) multiplier.}
