As on every other platform in this report series, the gap between library-backed and PUREPASCAL grows sharply with N for every O(N^3) operation, and Arm Performance Libraries proves to be a fully capable drop-in for both OpenBLAS's cblas_* role and (via the newly-added HAVE_LAPACKE wiring) MKL's LAPACKE_* role on this machine, despite Arm officially targeting Neoverse server cores rather than the Pi 5's Cortex-A76. MatMult sees the largest and most consistent speedups (97x-194x at N=1000 across the four types, ArmPL's cblas_?gemm vs a plain triple loop) - single/complex types pull further ahead than double real, consistent with PUREPASCAL's larger per-element constant-factor cost for those types. LinearSolve is the one operation with a genuine below-1.00x cell: real-double LinearSolve at N=100 is actually 4.3x }{\hich\af4\loch\i\f4\loch
slower}{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
 library-backed (0.23x "speedup", 7.69ms vs 1.79ms) before the library decisively pulls ahead again at N=1000 (22.83x) - the single/complex types show no such dip (2.09x-8.06x at N=100), so this looks like a genuine ArmPL lapacke_dgesv characteristic at this specific size/type rather than a general PUREPASCAL-favourable regime; not investigated further here. Invert lands in the middle at 3x-53x, tracking LinearSolve's LU cost plus an extra O(N^3) triangular-solve pass.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af8\afs24\alang1081 \ltrch\lang2057\langfe2052\hich\af3\loch\widctlpar\hyphpar1\ltrpar\cf0\f3\fs24\lang2057\kerning1\dbch\af10\langfe2052\ql\li0\ri0\lin0\rin0\fi0\sb0\sa180\ltrpar{\hich\af4\loch\f4\loch
FFT/IFFT show by far the largest speedups of anything in this report - up to 2331x for double-precision IFFT at N=1000 - even larger than the 685x seen on the Windows/Ryzen machine, because FFTW stays sub-0.1ms across the whole N=10..1000 range on this Pi while PPDirectDFT's/PPDirectDFTS's O(N^2) direct summation scales far worse on its weaker single-core throughput; this is the single most dramatic library-vs-PUREPASCAL gap measured on any platform in this series so far. EigDecompose shows modest, consistent gains (4.65x-5.75x at N=50/100, double and single alike) - noticeably better than the Windows report's own 0.85x-2.10x for the same operation, suggesting PurePascalEigHqr2's ported EISPACK QR-iteration carries a comparatively larger constant-factor penalty relative to ArmPL's LAPACKE_dgeev/sgeev on this chip than PurePascalLU?/Invert's simpler algorithm does relative to its own library counterpart.}
